}
}
\section{Which livestock proxy the weights come from}{

\code{proxy_method} selects the within-country weight, and every output row
records the resolved value in \code{method_livestock_proxy}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"luh2_area"}: LUH2 extent alone (\code{proxy_method = "luh2"}).
\item \code{"luh2_grass"}: LUH2 extent times grass NPP (\code{proxy_method = "luh2"}
with \code{grass_productivity} supplied). The grass weighting reaches the
\code{pasture} and \code{rangeland} proxies only, so \code{cropland} and \code{mixed}
groups in the same call stay \code{"luh2_area"}. The label is per species
group, not per cell: a grazer cell with no \code{grass_npp} keeps its area
weight but still travels under \code{"luh2_grass"}, because what the
column records is the weighting regime the group ran under.
\item \code{"glw3"}: GLW3 density masked by that year's LUH2 extent
(\code{proxy_method = "glw3"}).
}

The default stays \code{"luh2"} even though \code{"glw3"} is the better-informed
proxy. WHEP has no data mechanism for GLW3 yet -- no download script,
env var, pin or reader (whep#1000, task T15a-ii) -- so a \code{"glw3"}
default would abort every production run. This is a deliberate,
documented deviation from "the default is the most rigorous available
method", of the same shape as the interim \code{area_key = "grid"} default
in \code{R/spatialize_compartments.R}; whep#1000 task T20 is the gate that
revisits it.
}

\section{Species groups must be mapped, not guessed}{

Every \code{species_group} in \code{livestock_data} must have a row in
\code{species_proxy}; an unmapped group aborts naming it. It used to fall
back to the \code{"pasture"} proxy silently, so a typo or a new FAOSTAT item
was given a grazing distribution with no trace in the output.

The catch-all is explicit, not implicit.
\code{inst/extdata/livestock_mapping.csv} maps FAOSTAT items 1140 and 1150
(rabbits and hares, other rodents) and 1171 (live animals nes) onto the
group \code{"other"} with the \code{cropland} and \code{mixed} proxies, so a catch-all
group is reached by an explicit item mapping upstream and never by
name-matching here. A group carrying several proxies keeps the first,
as before.
}

\section{Which area code the output is keyed on}{

The chain allocates \emph{from} a national table keyed on \code{area_code} and
\emph{into} a \code{country_grid} keyed the same way, so both sides speak the raw
reporting vocabulary the grid was rasterized in. WHEP's polity-keyed
national tables are aggregated on \code{polity_area_code} instead, a bucket
that a reporting code need not equal: \code{276} Sudan and \code{277} South Sudan
both fall in bucket \code{206}. Every such output row therefore carries two
territorial keys that disagree, and whether a consumer joins on
\code{area_code} or on \code{polity_area_code} decides whether Sudan exists in its
result (whep#582).

\code{area_key} selects which of the two the output carries. It is not a
fallback: \code{"grid"} is the default, reproduces today's codes
bit-for-bit, and warns naming the codes that cannot join;
\code{"polity_area"} resolves each code to its bucket through
\link{polity_area_crosswalk} before the polity columns are attached, so
\code{area_code} and \code{polity_area_code} agree in every row. It respects
\code{options(whep.unfold_rest_of_world)} (see \code{\link[=folded_reporting_areas]{folded_reporting_areas()}}),
so the output and the national tables agree about where a Rest-of-World
member's rows belong.

Under \code{"polity_area"} the raw reporting code is \strong{carried, not
replaced}: the output gains \code{grid_area_code}, joined with \code{+} where two
reporting areas of one bucket meet in a cell and their rows collapse. So
the fold stays recoverable at the join rather than baked into the output,
the shape \code{\link[=build_cell_polity]{build_cell_polity()}} adopted for the same reason (whep#579).
}

